// @check
// @error a `type` declaration may not take the built-in type name `f64`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `pick` is inferred as `i32` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `x` is inferred as `f64` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `y` is inferred as `f64` — remove it to lean on inference
// A `type` DECLARATION MAY NOT TAKE A PRIMITIVE'S NAME.
//
// This program used to be ACCEPTED (`vl check` rc 0) and emit a module that does not
// instantiate. Graduated from `soundness/xfail-miscompile-prim-named-type-alias.vl`, whose
// own header called the reject "the fix" — it is filed here as the behavioural pin.
//
// THE TWO RESOLVERS DISAGREED, and neither was wrong on its own. The checker resolves a bare
// name through `tsLeafTy`, which asks `primTyOfName` FIRST — so `f64` in an annotation is the
// PRIMITIVE and `const x: f64 = 1.5` type-checks. Nothing screened the DECLARATION side:
// `udName` went into `cUnionNames` verbatim, so the emitter's alias registry believed `f64`
// named a literal union, and the value was stored as an f64 and read as the union's anyref
// box (`expected anyref, found f64`).
//
// EVERY PRIMITIVE SPELLING REPRODUCED IT, each mismatching in whichever direction that prim's
// rep differs from the atom box — `type i32` gave `expected anyref, found i32` and
// `type string` gave `expected i32, found (ref $type)`. The reject is `primTyOfName`, the one
// home of those spellings and the same function `tsLeafTy` asks, so it covers all of them and
// cannot drift from the resolution rule. `null` never reached here: the parser refuses it as a
// declaration name already.
//
// THE THREE `@hint`s ARE DECLARED RATHER THAN REMOVED, exactly as the xfail declared them:
// the annotations are what make the checker resolve `f64` to the PRIMITIVE, which is half of
// the disagreement. Leaning on inference would dissolve the fixture.
//
// A REJECT RATHER THAN A RESOLUTION CHANGE, because the declaration is unusable either way —
// the primitive shadows it at every annotation, so nothing can ever refer to it. Making one
// side win would silently redefine what a keyword means inside one file.
type f64 = "a" | "b"

function pick(): i32 {
  const x: f64 = 1.5
  const y: f64 = 2.5
  if x < y { return 1 }
  return 0
}

print(pick())
